  { q: 'You insert 30, then 20, then 10 into an empty AVL tree. Which case is this, and what is the fix?', opts: ['LL case: one right rotation at 30', 'RR case: one left rotation at 30', 'LR case: two rotations', 'No fix needed'], a: 0, why: 'From 30, the new value went left (20), then left (10): LL. A straight line to the left is fixed with one <b>right</b> rotation at 30. The new root is 20.' },
  { q: 'You insert 10, then 30, then 20. Which case is this?', opts: ['RR', 'RL', 'LR', 'LL'], a: 1, why: 'From 10, the new value went right (to 30), then left (to 20). Right, then left: <b>RL</b>. It is a zigzag, so it needs two rotations: right at 30, then left at 10.' },
  { q: 'After an insertion, two nodes have balance factor +2: one is the root, the other is lower down, closer to the new value. Which one do you fix?', opts: ['The root, always', 'The lower one, closest to the new value', 'Both, one rotation each', 'Neither; rebuild the tree'], a: 1, why: 'Fix the <b>first</b> unbalanced node you meet going up from the new value. After that one fix, the nodes above it become balanced too.' },
  { q: 'During a right rotation at node z (its left child is y), what happens to y\'s right child?', opts: ['It is deleted', 'It becomes z\'s left child', 'It becomes y\'s left child', 'It becomes the new root'], a: 1, why: 'y moves up and z moves down to y\'s right. y\'s old right child cannot stay (that spot now holds z), so it moves over to become z\'s left child. It still fits the BST rule there.' },
  { q: 'A plain BST and an AVL tree each get the values 1 to 1,000,000, inserted in sorted order. About how many steps can one search need in each?', opts: ['About 20 in both', 'Up to 1,000,000 in the BST; at most a few dozen (about 20 to 30) in the AVL tree', 'About 20 in the BST; up to 1,000,000 in the AVL tree', 'Up to 1,000,000 in both'], a: 1, why: 'Sorted input makes the plain BST a chain, so a search can pass every value: up to n steps, O(n). The AVL tree stays balanced, so its height grows like log₂ n: about 20 for a million values, O(log n). See <a href="#speed">How fast?</a> in Part 1.' },
  { q: 'Which traversal of any AVL tree gives the values in sorted order?', opts: ['Pre-order', 'In-order', 'Post-order', 'None; rotations mix up the order'], a: 1, why: 'An AVL tree is still a BST, and rotations never break the BST rule. So in-order (Left, Node, Right) is always sorted.' },
];
