---
<Base title="AVL Trees · ITC 120" description="AVL trees: balance factors, the four rotations, and step-by-step insertion you can play with." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Midterm › Balanced trees</p>
    <p class="kicker">Balanced trees · Lesson 1 of 2</p>
    <h1>AVL Trees</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">A binary search tree that fixes its own shape, so searching stays fast.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>explain why a BST built from sorted values is slow, using its height, and compare the time of search and insert in a balanced tree (about log₂ n steps) and an unbalanced one (up to n steps);</li>
        <li>compute the <b>height</b> and <b>balance factor</b> (left − right) of any node;</li>
        <li>name the case at an unbalanced node (<b>LL, RR, LR, RL</b>) and fix it with the right rotation(s);</li>
        <li>insert a list of values into an AVL tree <b>by hand</b> and write its traversals, the way the exam asks;</li>
        <li>point to the lines in the Java <code>insertRec</code> that update heights and fix each case.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the BST lessons (insertion and traversals), scratch paper, and a pen.</p>
  </header>

  <!-- 1 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="why">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The problem</p>
      <h2>Why a BST can become slow</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Recall how BST search works: at each node, you compare and go <b>left</b> (smaller) or <b>right</b> (larger). Each comparison takes you <b>one level down</b>. So the number of comparisons depends on how <span class="hl">tall</span> the tree is.</p>
      <p>But the shape of a BST depends on the <b>order</b> you insert the values. Below are the same seven values, 10 to 70, inserted in two different orders. Both trees follow the BST rule. Count the comparisons needed to find <b>70</b> (the shaded nodes):</p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <p class="fig-cap">Same values, different shapes</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <p class="small"><b>Inserted in sorted order:</b> 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70</p>
            <TreeFig values={chain} marks={{ 10: 'path', 20: 'path', 30: 'path', 40: 'path', 50: 'path', 60: 'path', 70: 'found' }} label="A BST shaped like a chain going down to the right" />
            <p class="small"><span class="chip">height 6</span> Finding 70 takes <b>7 comparisons</b>.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="small"><b>Inserted in mixed order:</b> 40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70</p>
            <TreeFig values={bushy} marks={{ 40: 'path', 60: 'path', 70: 'found' }} label="A bushy BST with 40 at the root" />
            <p class="small"><span class="chip">height 2</span> Finding 70 takes only <b>3 comparisons</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <p>The first tree is really just a linked list wearing a tree costume. With 1,000 values inserted in sorted order, a search could need 1,000 comparisons. A well-shaped tree with 1,000 values needs only about 10.</p>

      <div class="plain" id="speed">
        <p class="note-label">How fast? Balanced vs. unbalanced</p>
        <p>Search, insert, and delete each walk <b>one path down</b> the tree, so their work grows with the tree's <b>height</b>. Here is how the number of steps grows with n, the number of values:</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Tree</th><th>Height with n values</th><th>1,000 values</th><th>1,000,000 values</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Unbalanced BST</b> (worst case: a chain)</td><td>about n</td><td>up to 1,000 steps</td><td>up to 1,000,000 steps</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Balanced tree</b> (AVL, red-black)</td><td>close to log₂ n</td><td>about 10 steps</td><td>about 20 steps</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Where does "about 10" come from? In a balanced tree, each level can hold <b>twice</b> as many nodes as the level above it: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and so on. A search walks <b>one path</b> down, one node per level, so its number of steps is the number of levels:</p>
        <figure class="fig"><Fragment set:html={levelsPic} /><figcaption>Each level holds twice as many nodes as the one above. A search (orange) visits only one node per level.</figcaption></figure>
        <p>10 levels hold 1 + 2 + 4 + … + 512 = <b>1,023</b> nodes, about 1,000. 20 levels hold about 1,000,000. So in a balanced tree, a search takes only about 10 steps for 1,000 values, and about 20 for a million.</p>
        <p>Books call this number <b>log₂ n</b> ("log base 2 of n"): how many times you can cut n in half until you reach 1. 1,024 → 512 → 256 → 128 → 64 → 32 → 16 → 8 → 4 → 2 → 1 is <b>10</b> halvings, so log₂ 1,024 = 10. This is why balanced trees stay fast: doubling the number of values adds only <b>one</b> more level. 2,048 values need about 11 steps, not 2,048.</p>
        <p>Keeping the tree balanced does not slow things down either: the fix it uses, a <b>rotation</b> (you will learn it in Part 4), changes only a few links.</p>
        <p class="small muted">Good to know: books write the two speeds as <b>O(n)</b> ("order n") for the unbalanced worst case and <b>O(log n)</b> ("order log n") for a balanced tree. An AVL tree can be a little taller than log₂ n, but never much: at most about 1.44 × log₂ n. So even a million values need fewer than 30 steps.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Where you see this in IT</p>
        <p>Sorted input is very common: student ID numbers given out in order, receipts numbered 0001, 0002, 0003, or records saved with the date and time. Insert any of these into a plain BST and you get the slow chain.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sticky">
        <p class="note-label">Think of a pedicab</p>
        <p>A Mati pedicab has a front seat beside the driver and two benches at the back. If the back is full and the front is empty, the pedicab tilts backward: its bottom can nearly scrape the road, and the back wheels carry too much weight (worse in older pedicabs). So the driver asks a passenger to move to the front seat. Nobody gets off; they just change seats.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.5rem">An <b>AVL tree</b> does the same thing. When one side gets too heavy, it moves a few nodes around (a <b>rotation</b>). No value is removed, and the BST rule still holds.</p>
      </div>

      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={pedicabTilt()} /><figcaption>Top view (front at the top) and a side view. Before: all four passengers sit at the back, so the pedicab tilts backward. After: the passenger in orange moved to the front seat. Same four passengers, now balanced, like the same values after a rotation.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="balance-factor">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · Measuring the lean</p>
      <h2>Height and balance factor</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>To know if a tree is leaning, we need a number for each node. That number is built from <b>height</b>, which you learned in the trees lesson.</p>

      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Height (review)</p>
        <p>The <b>height of a node</b> is the number of edges on the <Peek label="longest path from that node down to a leaf" title="Height, in a picture" html={heightPeek} />.</p>
        <p>A leaf has height <b>0</b>. An empty spot (a <code>null</code> child) has height <b>−1</b>, because it is one level below a leaf.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Balance factor</p>
        <p>The <b>balance factor</b> of a node is the height of its left subtree minus the height of its right subtree:</p>
        <p class="formula">balance factor = height(left) − height(right)</p>
      </div>

      <p>Work it out for <b>every node</b>, one node at a time, in three steps:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Find the height of the node's <b>left subtree</b> (that is, the height of its left child). No left child? Use <b>−1</b>.</li>
        <li>Find the height of its <b>right subtree</b> the same way. No right child? Use <b>−1</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Subtract:</b> left − right.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Do not mix up the two numbers. A node's <b>height</b> says how tall its own subtree is. Its <b>balance factor</b> compares its two sides. In the pictures below, <code>h=</code> under a node is its height, and the small number at its top right is its balance factor.</p>

      <p>The sign of the balance factor tells you which way the node leans. There are three possible readings:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Balance factor</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>positive</b> (+1, +2, …)</td><td>The <b>left</b> side is taller.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>0</b></td><td>Both sides are the same height.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>negative</b> (−1, −2, …)</td><td>The <b>right</b> side is taller.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">The balance rule</p>
        <p>A node is <b>balanced</b> if its balance factor is <b>−1, 0, or +1</b>.</p>
        <p>It is <b>unbalanced</b> when it reaches <b>+2 or −2</b>. That is when an AVL tree (the self-fixing tree from Part 1) must fix it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Example: work it out</h3>
        <TreeFig values={[40, 20, 60, 10, 30]} showH showBf label="A tree with heights and balance factors shown" />
        <p>Look at node <b>20</b>: its left child 10 has height 0, and its right child 30 has height 0. So its balance factor is 0 − 0 = <b>0</b>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.5rem">Look at node <b>40</b>: its left subtree (starting at 20) has height 1. Its right subtree (starting at 60) has height 0. So 1 − 0 = <b>+1</b>. It leans left a little, but that is allowed.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.5rem">Look at leaf <b>60</b>: both children are empty (height −1). So −1 − (−1) = <b>0</b>. Every leaf has balance factor 0.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Example 2: a missing child counts as −1</h3>
        <p>Insert 10, 20, 30 into a plain BST. You get this chain (you will meet it again in Part 4 as the <b>RR case</b>):</p>
        <TreeFig values={[10, 20, 30]} showH showBf minW={220} label="A chain: 10 at the top, 20 its right child, 30 the right child of 20. Heights: 10 is 2, 20 is 1, 30 is 0. Balance factors: 10 is −2, 20 is −1, 30 is 0." />
        <p>Work from the bottom up. The table shows each node's left height, right height, and balance factor:</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Node</th><th>Left subtree height</th><th>Right subtree height</th><th>Balance factor</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>30</b></td><td>−1 (no left child)</td><td>−1 (no right child)</td><td>−1 − (−1) = <b>0</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>20</b></td><td>−1 (no left child)</td><td>0 (30 is a leaf)</td><td>−1 − 0 = <b>−1</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>10</b></td><td>−1 (no left child)</td><td>1 (20 → 30 is one edge)</td><td>−1 − 1 = <b>−2</b></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Node 10 is <b>−2</b>: unbalanced, because its right side is two levels taller than its left. Notice that 10's balance factor (−2) is not its height (2): the height measures one side going down, the balance factor compares two sides.</p>
      </div>

      <BfExplorer id="bfx" values={[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 70, 5, 80]} />

      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Exam traps</p>
        <ul>
          <li>It is <b>left minus right</b>, not right minus left. Getting the order wrong flips every sign.</li>
          <li>It uses <b>heights</b>, not the number of nodes.</li>
          <li>Check <b>every</b> node. A tree can be fine at the root but unbalanced lower down (like 60 in the explorer above: −2, while the root 40 is 0).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="avl">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · The idea</p>
      <h2>What an AVL tree is</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">AVL tree</p>
        <p>An <b>AVL tree</b> is a binary search tree in which every node has a balance factor of −1, 0, or +1. After every insertion or deletion, it checks the balance factors and uses <b>rotations</b> to fix any node that reaches ±2.</p>
      </div>
      <p>So an AVL tree follows two rules at the same time:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><b>The BST rule:</b> smaller values to the left, larger values to the right.</li>
        <li><b>The balance rule:</b> every node's balance factor is −1, 0, or +1.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>It is named after the two people who invented it in 1962, <b>G. M. <u>A</u>delson-<u>V</u>elsky</b> and <b>E. M. <u>L</u>andis</b>. It was the first self-balancing binary search tree.</p>

      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Exam trap</p>
        <p>An AVL tree is <b>not</b> perfectly balanced. Small differences (±1) are allowed. It does not rotate after every insert, only when a node reaches ±2.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="rotations">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · The fix</p>
      <h2>Rotations</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>A <b>rotation</b> is a small rearrangement of a node and one of its children. One goes up, the other goes down, and the shape changes. The BST rule still holds afterwards, so the in-order list stays the same.</p>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>A right rotation, slowly</h3>
        <p>Here is a <b>right rotation at 40</b>. Its left child, 30, moves up into 40's spot. 40 moves down to become 30's right child.</p>
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="two-col rot">
            <div>
              <p class="small"><b>Before</b></p>
              <TreeFig values={[40, 30, 50, 20, 35]} marks={{ 40: 'down', 30: 'pivot', 35: 'moved' }} minW={260} label="Before: 40 at the top, 30 on its left, 35 is 30's right child" />
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="small"><b>After right rotation at 40</b></p>
              <TreeFig values={[30, 20, 40, 35, 50]} marks={{ 40: 'down', 30: 'pivot', 35: 'moved' }} minW={260} label="After: 30 at the top, 40 on its right, 35 is now 40's left child" />
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="legend">
            <span><i class="l-pivot"></i> moves up</span>
            <span><i style="border-style:dashed;border-color:var(--teal)"></i> moves down</span>
            <span><i class="l-moved"></i> changes parent</span>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p>It happens in three moves:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>30 moves up</b> into 40's old spot.</li>
          <li><b>40 moves down</b> to become 30's right child.</li>
          <li>But 30's right spot already held <b>35</b>. Now 40 is there, so <span class="hl">35 needs a new spot</span>.</li>
        </ol>
        <figure class="fig">
          <TreeFig values={[30, 20, 40, 50]} marks={{ 40: 'down', 30: 'pivot' }} showNil spot={{ key: 40, side: 'l' }} minW={260} label="Halfway: 30 at the top, 20 on its left, 40 on its right, 50 under 40 on the right. 40's left spot is empty and highlighted: that is where 35 goes." />
          <figcaption>After moves 1 and 2, 35 is waiting for a spot. The highlighted box is 40's empty left spot: 40 lost its left child (30) when 30 moved up.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Where can 35 go? It must be on <b>30's right</b> (35 is bigger than 30) and on <b>40's left</b> (35 is smaller than 40). The highlighted spot is the only one that is both, so there is exactly <b>one</b> correct place: <b>35 becomes 40's left child</b>.</p>
        <p>Check: the in-order list before is 20, 30, 35, 40, 50. After, it is still 20, 30, 35, 40, 50.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>A left rotation</h3>
        <p>A <b>left rotation</b> is the same three moves with left and right swapped. Here is a <b>left rotation at 20</b>:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Its <b>right</b> child, <b>30</b>, moves up into 20's spot.</li>
          <li><b>20 moves down</b> to become 30's <b>left</b> child.</li>
          <li>30's old left child, <b>25</b>, moves to 20's empty <b>right</b> spot (25 is bigger than 20 and smaller than 30).</li>
        </ol>
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="two-col rot">
            <div>
              <p class="small"><b>Before</b></p>
              <TreeFig values={[20, 10, 30, 25, 40]} marks={{ 20: 'down', 30: 'pivot', 25: 'moved' }} minW={260} label="Before: 20 at the top, 10 on its left, 30 on its right, 25 is 30's left child, 40 is 30's right child" />
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="small"><b>After left rotation at 20</b></p>
              <TreeFig values={[30, 20, 40, 10, 25]} marks={{ 20: 'down', 30: 'pivot', 25: 'moved' }} minW={260} label="After: 30 at the top, 20 on its left with children 10 and 25, 40 on its right" />
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p>Check: the in-order list is 10, 20, 25, 30, 40 before and after.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The four cases</h3>
        <p>When a node becomes unbalanced, we look at the path from that node toward the value we just inserted. The first two moves on that path give the case its name. There are four types of cases:</p>
        <div class="scroll-x"><table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Path from the unbalanced node</th><th>Shape</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>LL</b></td><td>left, then left</td><td>straight line</td><td>1 right rotation at the unbalanced node</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>RR</b></td><td>right, then right</td><td>straight line</td><td>1 left rotation at the unbalanced node</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>LR</b></td><td>left, then right</td><td>zigzag</td><td>left rotation at the child, then right rotation at the unbalanced node</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>RL</b></td><td>right, then left</td><td>zigzag</td><td>right rotation at the child, then left rotation at the unbalanced node</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <div class="remember">
          <p class="note-label">How to remember</p>
          <p><b>Straight line → 1 rotation. Zigzag → 2 rotations.</b></p>
          <p>For LL and RR, rotate the <b>opposite</b> way: too heavy on the left, rotate right. For LR and RL, the first rotation straightens the zigzag into a line, then you fix the line.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <AvlLab id="lab-cases" cap="Watch each case" hint="Pick a case, then tap Next step. Each example inserts three values into an empty tree." presets={[
        { label: 'LL', values: [30, 20, 10] },
        { label: 'RR', values: [10, 20, 30] },
        { label: 'LR', values: [30, 10, 20] },
        { label: 'RL', values: [10, 30, 20] },
      ]} />

      <p>Notice that all four examples end with the same tree: 20 at the top, 10 on the left, 30 on the right. Different starting shapes, same balanced result.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="insert">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Putting it together</p>
      <h2>Inserting, step by step</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Every AVL insertion follows the same four steps. This is the procedure you will use in the exam:</p>
      <ol class="procedure">
        <li><b>Insert</b> the value like a normal BST. It becomes a new leaf.</li>
        <li><b>Check</b> balance factors, starting from the new leaf and going up to the root.</li>
        <li>At the <b>first</b> node that reaches +2 or −2, <b>name the case</b> (LL, RR, LR, or RL) using the path toward the new value.</li>
        <li><b>Rotate</b> using the fix for that case. One fix is enough: the whole tree is balanced again.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If no node reaches ±2 in step 2, there is nothing to fix. Move on to the next value.</p>

      <AvlLab id="lab-full" cap="Worked example: all four cases" hint="This list triggers LL, LR, RR, and RL. Tap Next step and say the next move out loud before you tap. Then try your own numbers." custom presets={[
        { label: 'Worked example', values: [30, 65, 20, 15, 25, 10, 50, 60, 90, 45] },
      ]} />

      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Doing it on paper</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Redraw the whole tree</b> after every insertion that causes a rotation. Do not erase and squeeze.</li>
          <li>Write the balance factor <b>beside each node</b> on the path you just walked.</li>
          <li>Before rotating, write the case name (for example, <b>"LR at 65"</b>). It keeps you from rotating the wrong way.</li>
          <li>After rotating, do a quick in-order check: the list must still be sorted.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Exam traps</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Name the case from the <b>unbalanced node</b>, not from the root.</li>
          <li>Fix only the <b>lowest</b> unbalanced node (closest to the new value). After that one fix (one or two rotations), the nodes above it are balanced too. This is true for <b>insertion</b>; deleting a value can need more fixes higher up.</li>
          <li>Do not forget the child that <b>changes parent</b> during a rotation. Losing it is the most common mistake.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="paper">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Try it on paper</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>The exam asks you to build an AVL tree and then traverse it. Do each problem on paper <b>before</b> opening the answer. Then check yourself with <a href="#lab-full">the lab in Part 5</a> by typing in the same numbers.</p>
      {practice.map((p, i) => (
        <div class="paper flow">
          <p class="note-label">Problem {i + 1}</p>
          <p>Insert these values, in order, into an empty AVL tree:</p>
          <p class="mono seq">{p.values.join(', ')}</p>
          <p>Then write the <b>pre-order</b>, <b>in-order</b>, and <b>post-order</b> traversals of your final tree.</p>
          <details class="reveal">
            <summary>Show the answer</summary>
            <div class="flow">
              <TreeFig values={p.values} mode="avl" showBf label={`Final AVL tree for problem ${i + 1}`} />
              <p set:html={p.rotations} />
              <table class="tbl">
                <tbody>
                  <tr><th>Pre-order</th><td class="mono">{p.pre.join(', ')}</td></tr>
                  <tr><th>In-order</th><td class="mono">{p.ino.join(', ')}</td></tr>
                  <tr><th>Post-order</th><td class="mono">{p.post.join(', ')}</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="java">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 7 · In code</p>
      <h2>AVL insertion in Java</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>The Java version is your <code>insertRec</code> from the <a href={base + '/itc120/recursion/'}>Recursion</a> lesson, plus a few small methods. The node gets one new field, <code>height</code>, so we do not have to count the height again every time.</p>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>1. The node remembers its height</h3>
<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(code1)} /></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>2. Three small helpers</h3>
<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(code2)} /></pre>
        <p>These match <Peek label="Part 2" title="From Part 2" html={bfPeek} /> exactly: a leaf is 0, an empty spot is −1, and the balance factor is left minus right.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>3. The right rotation</h3>
<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(code3)} /></pre>
        <p>Compare <code>rotateRight</code> with the <Peek label="right rotation picture in Part 4" title="From Part 4" html={rotPeek} />: <code>z</code> is 40, <code>y</code> is 30, and <code>moved</code> is 35. The method <b>returns the new top</b>, so whoever called it can connect it back to the tree.</p>
        <p><code>rotateLeft</code> is the same code with every left and right swapped (a mirror image), and <code>insertRec</code> below calls it too. You will write it yourself in <a href="#task">the coding task at the end of this part</a>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>4. Insert, then fix on the way back up</h3>
<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(code4)} /></pre>
        <p>You call it the same way as before: <code>root = insertRec(root, 25);</code></p>
      </div>

      <div class="plain">
        <p class="note-label">Why "on the way back up" works</p>
        <p>Recursion goes <b>down</b> to find the spot, then each call finishes in reverse order, from the new leaf <b>back up</b> to the root. That is exactly step 2 of the procedure: check from the new node upward. The first call to find <code>b</code> equal to 2 or −2 is the lowest unbalanced node, and it fixes itself before returning.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.5rem">How does the code name the case? <code>b &gt; 1</code> means the left side is too tall (first letter <b>L</b>). Then <code>value &lt; node.left.data</code> means the new value went left again (second letter <b>L</b>), and so on.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow" id="task">
        <p class="note-label">Your coding task: write rotateLeft</p>
        <p>A left rotation fixes the RR case, when the right side is too tall. Look at <Peek label="a left rotation" title="A left rotation at 20" html={rotLPeek} />: <code>z</code> is 20, <code>y</code> is 30, and <code>moved</code> is 25. <code>y</code> moves up, <code>z</code> goes down to the left, and <code>moved</code> changes parent. In code, it is <code>rotateRight</code> with every <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> swapped.</p>
        <p>Write <code>rotateLeft</code> in about 7 lines where it says YOUR CODE. The rest of the program is the code from this part: <code>rotateRight</code>, the three helpers, and <code>insertRec</code>, which calls <code>rotateLeft</code> in the RR, LR, and RL cases. Each test prints a tree's pre-order and its height. Run it: each printed line must match its comment.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskCode)} /></pre>
        <TryIt />
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show one possible answer</summary><div class="flow">
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskAns)} /></pre>
          <p>Output, one per line: <code>20 10 30 height 1</code>, <code>20 10 30 height 1</code>, <code>40 20 10 30 60 50 70 height 2</code>, <code>50 25 20 30 70 height 2</code>.</p>
          <p>Put it beside <code>rotateRight</code>: <code>z.left</code> became <code>z.right</code>, and <code>y.right</code> became <code>y.left</code>. The two <code>updateHeight</code> lines stay the same, in the same order.</p>
          <p>The tests reach <code>rotateLeft</code> in different ways. 10, 20, 30 is the RR case: one left rotation at 10. 10, 30, 20 is the RL case: a right rotation at 30, then a left rotation at 10. Inserting 10 to 70 in order needs four left rotations, the same as Problem 2, and ends as a perfect tree of height 2. The last test rotates at 20, below the root: the new top, 25, is returned and saved as 50's left child.</p>
          <p>A common mistake is updating <code>y</code> before <code>z</code>. Then <code>y</code>'s height is counted from <code>z</code>'s <b>old</b> height, from when <code>z</code> was on top, so it comes out too big. The first test prints <code>20 10 30 height 3</code> for a tree of only three nodes. Wrong heights give wrong balance factors, so later rotations go wrong too: 10 to 70 ends as <code>60 40 20 10 30 50 70 height 5</code>. Update <code>z</code> first, because it is lower now and <code>y</code>'s height depends on it.</p>
        </div></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 8 · Check yourself</p>
      <h2>Self-check</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Not graded. Tap an answer to see if you got it, and read why. Try again until you get them all.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-avl" questions={quiz} />

      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li>A BST can become a slow chain if values arrive in sorted order: up to n steps per search. A balanced tree needs only about log₂ n.</li>
          <li>Balance factor = height(left) − height(right). Allowed: −1, 0, +1. Fix at ±2.</li>
          <li>An AVL tree is a BST that keeps every balance factor in −1 to +1 using rotations.</li>
          <li>Name the case from the unbalanced node: LL and RR need 1 rotation; LR and RL need 2.</li>
          <li>During a rotation, one child changes parent. Don't lose it.</li>
          <li>Rotations never break the BST rule, so in-order is always sorted.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <p><b>Next lesson:</b> <a href={base + '/itc120/red-black-trees/'}>Red-black trees</a>, another way to keep a BST balanced.</p>

      <div class="refs small muted">
        <p class="note-label">References</p>
        <ol>
          <li>G. M. Adelson-Velsky and E. M. Landis, "An algorithm for the organization of information," <i>Soviet Mathematics Doklady</i>, vol. 3, pp. 1259–1263, 1962.</li>
          <li>Programiz, "AVL Tree." <a href="https://www.programiz.com/dsa/avl-tree">programiz.com/dsa/avl-tree</a></li>
          <li>T. H. Cormen, C. E. Leiserson, R. L. Rivest, and C. Stein, <i>Introduction to Algorithms</i>, 4th ed. MIT Press, 2022 (rotations, Section 13.2).</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
